# Repository Refactoring Rules: Joy of Building Pivot

This repository is being refactored from a commercial product into an open-source, non-commercial hobby experiment celebrating pure building, learning, and creative exploration. 

When generating, editing, or refactoring code in this repository, strictly adhere to the following rules:

## 1. Remove All Commercial & Payment Mechanisms
- **Delete Stripe / Payment Logic:** Remove all dependencies, SDKs, webhook handlers, and API integrations for payment processors (Stripe, RevenueCat, PayPal, Lemonsqueezy, etc.).
- **Remove Paywalls & Gating:** Convert any gated, "Pro", or premium features into fully unlocked, free functionality.
- **Clean UI Elements:** Remove pricing cards, subscription plans, upgrade prompts, "Buy Now" buttons, and checkout flows.
- **Replace Monetization CTAs:** Replace former purchase links with:
  - An "Open Source" link pointing to the GitHub repository.
  - A "Play Demo" or "Try App" action button.

## 2. Insert AS-IS Disclaimers & Legal Notice
- **Footer Disclaimer:** Ensure every page layout includes an "As-Is" legal disclaimer in the footer or modal:
  > *"This app is a non-commercial hobby experiment provided 'AS IS' without warranty of any kind, express or implied. Use at your own risk."*
- **Infinite Scroll / UI Well-being (if applicable):** If the application features continuous feed mechanics, include:
  > *"Built for fun and curiosity. Take regular breaks and enjoy building!"*

## 3. Licensing & Open Source Artifacts
- **MIT License:** Ensure a standard `LICENSE` file (MIT License) exists at the root of the repository. Include the default non-warranty clause: *"THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED 'AS IS', WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND..."*
- **README Refactoring:** Update `README.md` to explain:
  - What concept or tech stack was explored during this build.
  - Key lessons learned and "Why I Built This".
  - How to run the app locally (`npm run dev` / setup steps).
  - Explicit mention that this is part of #BuildForJoy / non-commercial exploration.

## 4. Code Quality & Maintenance
- Do not introduce new serverless backend overhead or complex database calls unless required for local execution.
- Maintain existing visual styles, micro-animations, and UI components while stripping commercial copy.